<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wade in the Water</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words first published <a href="/years/1901">1901</a>; first commercial recording <a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Wade in the Water&quot; is an African American spiritual. Its words were first printed in 1901 in New Jubilee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, published by John Wesley Work Jr. and his brother Frederick J. Work. Work, also known as John Work II (1871–1925), spent three decades at Fisk University, the historically black college in Nashville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, collecting the songs of the original Fisk Jubilee Singers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Steal Away&quot; is counted, with &quot;Swing Low, Sweet Chariot&quot;, &quot;<b>Wade in the Water</b>&quot; and &quot;The Gospel Train&quot;, among the spirituals said to carry hidden codes: besides their faith in God, messages telling enslaved people to run away, on their own or by the Underground Railroad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Wade in the Water</b>&quot; is an African American spiritual.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
